<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle on the Marchfeld</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1278">1278</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle on the Marchfeld, also called the Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen, was fought on 26 August 1278 on the plain north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf I of Germany</a>, allied with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary">Ladislaus IV of Hungary</a>, defeated and killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a>. The victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, who ruled Austria until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld.jpg" alt="Battle on the Marchfeld" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mór Than · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IV_L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_%C3%A9s_Rudolf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of Emperor Frederick II in 1250, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> fell into crisis. In that year Ottokar II, son of King Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, moved into the Duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, which had no duke, and was proclaimed duke by the estates. He became king of Bohemia in 1253, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> at Kressenbrunn in 1260, and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> through an inheritance contract with Ulrich III of Carinthia. At the height of his power he aimed at the imperial crown, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> chose Rudolph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> on 29 September 1273.</p><p>Ottokar did not acknowledge Rudolph and failed to appear before the diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 1275, so he was placed under the imperial ban. Rudolph gathered allies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meinhard-ii-of-gorizia-tyrol">Meinhard II of Gorizia-Tyrol</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary">Ladislaus IV of Hungary</a>. Besieged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1276, Ottokar renounced his acquisitions and held only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. In 1278 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> again, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-xiii-duke-of-bavaria">Henry I of Lower Bavaria</a>, who had switched sides, and besieged Laa an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaya">Thaya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Ottokar fielded 6,000 cavalry, of whom 1,000 were heavily armed. Rudolph had 300 heavy and 4,000 light cavalry together with 5,000 Cuman horse archers. On 26 August the armies met near Dürnkrut. Cuman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> harassed the Bohemian left wing from the early morning, but in the main clash Ottokar's larger force seemed to gain the upper hand, and Rudolph, then sixty, was unhorsed and narrowly escaped. After three hours the Bohemian knights in heavy armour suffered in the summer heat.</p><p>At noon Rudolph sent a fresh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> force hidden behind hills and woods against Ottokar's right flank. Such ambushes were regarded as dishonourable, and its commander Ulrich von Kapellen is said to have apologised to his men beforehand. The attack split the Bohemians. When Ottokar tried to bring up a reserve behind von Kapellen's men, his own troops took the move for flight, and the army collapsed. Ottokar's camp was plundered and he was found slain. No exact casualty figures exist, but his losses were far higher than Rudolph's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Rudolph had Ottokar's body displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. At the 1282 Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> he installed his sons Albert and Rudolf II as dukes of Austria, and their descendants held the title until 1918. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> he acted cautiously, agreeing with the nobility and Ottokar's widow Kunigunda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> that her son Wenceslaus II would succeed. The influence of the Přemyslid kings was confined to Bohemia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, and the battle marked the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> ascendancy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle on the Marchfeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His refusal to recognise Rudolf of Habsburg as King of the Romans cost him Austria in 1276 and ended in his defeat and death at the <b>Battle on the Marchfeld</b> in 1278.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards Béla IV encouraged stone castles, which helped repel a second Mongol invasion in 1285–1286, and in 1278 Ladislaus IV's cavalry helped Rudolf of Habsburg defeat Ottokar II of Bohemia on the <b>Marchfeld</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol raiders crossed it in 1241, and in 1278 Ottokar II of Bohemia, who had used Moravia as a base for his Austrian ambitions, was killed fighting Rudolf of Habsburg at the <b>Battle on the Marchfeld</b>, just beyond the Moravian border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the line died out in 1246, Ottokar II of Bohemia took Austria, but in 1278 Rudolf I of Habsburg defeated him at the <b>Marchfeld</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
